# The 2026-08-24 bindings. Listed BY NAME rather than as `anonlib/*.so`,
# keeping this file's existing shape: a glob would also swallow a .so that
# someone genuinely meant to track, and the explicit list is what makes a
# forgotten binding show up as untracked instead of vanishing silently.
anonlib/_anonlib_svm.so
anonlib/_anonlib_solver.so
anonlib/_anonlib_metrics.so
anonlib/_anonlib_preprocessing.so
anonlib/_anonlib_tsa.so
anonlib/_anonlib_linalg.so
# The 2026-09-01 bindings. BOTH WERE MISSING and the older one had already
# been COMMITTED as a 625K binary in `cc269dca` before anybody noticed --
# which is this file's own comment above coming true. The explicit list makes
# a forgotten binding show up as untracked; it only works if somebody adds
# the line when the binding lands, and twice in a row nobody did. Untracked
# forward in the commit that added these lines, not rewritten out of history.
anonlib/_anonlib_arima.so
anonlib/_anonlib_training.so
# The gp binding (2026-09-01, the thirteenth). Third instance of the same
# omission this file's comments predict: the binding shipped green and the
# .so sat untracked until the phase-8 rent pre-flight refused the dirty
# tree. Added the same day, forward.
anonlib/_anonlib_gp.so
# The mamba binding (2026-09-01, the fourteenth). FOURTH instance: the
# surface lane shipped green the same evening the gp line above was added
# for the third instance, and still forgot its own line. Found the next
# morning by the same signal as always -- the .so untracked in status.
anonlib/_anonlib_mamba.so
# The transformer binding (2026-09-02, the fifteenth). FIFTH instance of the
# same omission: the binding landed on 2026-09-02 and this line did not, so
# the first build left `python/anonlib/_anonlib_transformer.so` sitting
# untracked in `git status` where a careless add would have committed a
# 12 MB Mach-O. Added the day the binding first compiled.
anonlib/_anonlib_transformer.so
# Build outputs copied in by packaging/macos/build_release_wheel.sh so that
# `python -m anonlib verify` has a comparator inside the wheel and
# `python -m anonlib identity` has the harness, the three training GPU
# columns and the commit witness. The single sources stay in git:
# tools/identity_trace_diff.py, tools/identity_break.py and the columns
# python/anonlib/host_surface.py names under bench/results/identity_break/.
anonlib/_identity_trace_diff.py
anonlib/_identity_break.py
anonlib/_identity_lane_*.py
anonlib/identity_columns/
anonlib/identical/
anonlib/.dylibs/
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/

# the byte LM host build writes its .so here (bindings/build_byte_lm_host.sh);
# the sibling identical/ directory is already ignored above
anonlib/host/*.so

# Copies of the checked-in GPU-trained CTR verification fixtures.
anonlib/verify_reference/ctr_models/
# The Expose D bindings (2026-09-14). Fourth instance: they built IDENTICAL
# only, under anonlib/identical/, until classical FAST (2026-09-25) put the
# FAST builds in the package directory, where the 0.8.19 release-check saw
# them as changed paths it could not attribute. Embedding stays IDENTICAL only
# and is listed so a future FAST build cannot repeat this.
anonlib/_anonlib_kernel_methods.so
anonlib/_anonlib_mixture.so
anonlib/_anonlib_hdbscan.so
anonlib/_anonlib_resample.so
anonlib/_anonlib_ivf.so
anonlib/_anonlib_embedding.so
# The algorithm expansion's GPU bindings (lane/algos-prep, 2026-09-27): one per
# lane, named `_anonlib_x_<lane>` by host_surface.expansion_gpu_binding.
anonlib/_anonlib_x_*.so
anonlib/_anonlib_byte_lm.so
